        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "As described, this issue is a bit tricky. Mostly because I don't think this is a 'mini.jump2d' issue per se.\n\n> 1. An Oil buffer with a minihues colorscheme: The files can be [dimmed](https://github.com/stevearc/oil.nvim/blob/b73018b75affd13fa38e2fc94ef753b465f770d7/lua/oil/init.lua#L903), the directories however can not. See video.\n\nI would assume that this is an 'oil.nvim' issue because it doesn't seem to set extmark priorities. Explicitly setting a priority [here](https://github.com/stevearc/oil.nvim/blob/b73018b75affd13fa38e2fc94ef753b465f770d7/lua/oil/util.lua#L371-L373) looks like solves the issue.\n\nThe behavior that no explicit extmark priority means very high priority is unfortunate, but it is what it is. There is also no guidance about which priority values are allowed and/or recommended except `For example treesitter highlighting uses a value of 100.`.\n\nThe value of 4096 is not documented anywhere, so I am hesitant to use it. Especially values higher than that (even knowing that the Nightly multicursor seems to use it). The value of 999 in 'mini.jump2d' is the reasonable \"very high\" priority when all that is know is that \"tree-sitter highlighting has 100\".\n \n> 2. A MiniFiles buffer with a minihues colorscheme: Both directories and files cannot be dimmed.\n\nThis is an issue similar to 'oil.nvim': no explicit `priority` is set. This should be fixed by setting something like 101 and 102 priorities to icon and name highlighting.\n\n> 3. A MiniFiles buffer with the 'thorn' colorscheme: See example 1, because 'thorn' does not [define](https://github.com/jpwol/thorn.nvim/blob/e7074d253db38d76c5b709ac29f22c5e508da9d3/lua/thorn/groups/mini.lua#L21) the corresponding MiniFiles highlights. Thus, [MiniFilesFile](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/blob/6664ea9af6c43dc31934e27476bbe61c556c8dc1/lua/mini/files.lua#L1401) is cleared by the mini.files plugin, and can be dimmed.\n\nThis is expected, since if there is no highlight group defined there'll be nothing higher priority than `MiniJump2d` dimming.\n\n> 4. A buffer with colorcodes highlighted with mini.hipatterns. Characters in the pattern cannot be dimmed.\n\nWith default priority of 200, those *do* get dimmed. It is not always easy to see since dimming becomes subtle if displayed color is too vibrant. It can be checked by setting `MiniJump2d` foreground and displayed color (via background) to be the same. When dimming, the text of the color will disappear.\n\n---\n\nSo all in all, I think this is 'stevearc/oil.nvim' and 'mini.files' issues, not 'mini.jump2d'. Do you have time to make a PR for 'mini.files'?",
